1
00:00:00,125 --> 00:00:00,562
For Bach,

2
00:00:00,962 --> 00:00:03,262
AI is much more than automated statistics.

3
00:00:03,688 --> 00:00:06,588
He sees it as humanity's last great philosophical

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:07,150
project.

5
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:09,225
Intelligence, at its core,

6
00:00:09,638 --> 00:00:11,950
is the ability to make models of reality.

7
00:00:12,362 --> 00:00:13,925
And the real goal of AI is a

8
00:00:13,938 --> 00:00:15,638
system that can model itself,

9
00:00:16,012 --> 00:00:18,225
a mind that finally understands what a mind

10
00:00:18,238 --> 00:00:18,550
is.

11
00:00:18,962 --> 00:00:19,650
Seen this way,

12
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,150
the Turing test is not really a test

13
00:00:22,162 --> 00:00:23,412
of how clever machines are.

14
00:00:23,825 --> 00:00:25,388
It is a recursive test of us.

15
00:00:25,812 --> 00:00:28,487
Are human beings intelligent enough to understand their

16
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:30,238
own minds well enough to build one?

17
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:32,088
If we can rebuild ourselves,

18
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:34,225
we have finally understood ourselves.

19
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:36,338
But instead of building a wise mind,

20
00:00:36,712 --> 00:00:38,575
we have spent the last decade building a

21
00:00:38,588 --> 00:00:39,137
sick one.

22
00:00:39,575 --> 00:00:42,275
Bach compares social media to a global brain,

23
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:45,700
billions of people wired together into one giant

24
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:46,225
network.

25
00:00:46,638 --> 00:00:47,675
And what does that brain do?

26
00:00:48,088 --> 00:00:49,688
It has no stable sense of what is

27
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:50,125
real,

28
00:00:50,525 --> 00:00:52,400
no brakes, no one in charge.

29
00:00:52,838 --> 00:00:55,275
He describes Twitter as a global brain in

30
00:00:55,288 --> 00:00:56,700
a kind of permanent seizure,

31
00:00:56,988 --> 00:00:59,162
constantly firing and never settling.

32
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:02,188
Each of us becomes a little reinforcement learning

33
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:04,525
agent, chasing the reward of likes,

34
00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:06,750
outrage and empty arguments.

35
00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:08,413
A tiny hit of dopamine,

36
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:09,812
again and again.

37
00:01:10,238 --> 00:01:12,150
The network learns what keeps us scrolling,

38
00:01:12,512 --> 00:01:13,575
not what keeps us sane.

39
00:01:13,988 --> 00:01:14,538
Meanwhile,

40
00:01:14,925 --> 00:01:15,950
in the physical world,

41
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:18,662
we use clever accounting to drain the planet,

42
00:01:18,975 --> 00:01:20,150
and we call it innovation.

43
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:22,200
A wise global mind would look at the

44
00:01:22,212 --> 00:01:24,762
slope we are skiing down and change course.

45
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:27,912
Our current global mind is too busy refreshing

46
00:01:27,925 --> 00:01:28,425
its feed.

47
00:01:28,838 --> 00:01:30,962
To understand why we behave this way,

48
00:01:31,350 --> 00:01:32,550
Bach goes much deeper,

49
00:01:32,975 --> 00:01:34,900
all the way down to the smallest unit

50
00:01:34,912 --> 00:01:35,450
of life.
